1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
A binary operation on a set is a rule that takes two elements and returns one. Write every answer in a table and the rule is completely known.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Here is subtraction modulo 3 on the set 0, 1, 2. Row 1 column 2 means 1 minus 2, which is minus 1, which is 2 modulo 3.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Closure is the first promise: every cell must hold an element of the same set. All nine cells do, so this rule stays inside the set.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:30,000
The table is the whole object. Every later word — semigroup, monoid, group — is a promise about which pattern this table must contain.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Associativity says bracketing does not matter. Take three elements, combine the first two, then combine the result with the third.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Now the other bracketing. Zero minus 1 is 2, then 2 minus 2 is 0. But zero minus the answer of 1 minus 2 gives 1. The two cells disagree.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Addition modulo 6 passes. All 216 triples were computed and every pair of bracketings landed on the same cell.

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Closed plus associative is called a semigroup. That is the first named rung of the ladder, and nothing more is promised.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
An identity element leaves everything unchanged. In the table it is the row that copies the column headings, and the column that copies the row headings.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
There can be at most one. If e and f are both identities then e equals e star f, and e star f equals f, so the two names are one element.

11
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:24,000
A semigroup with an identity is a monoid. Addition modulo 6 is a monoid, multiplication modulo 6 is a monoid, and so are the strings under concatenation with the empty string.

12
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
So the ladder so far reads: closed, then associative, then an identity. Two more rungs remain, and the next one is the one that decides most exam questions.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
An inverse of a is an element b with a star b equal to the identity and b star a equal to the identity. In the table, the identity cell must appear in the row of a.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Multiplication modulo 6 fails on four rows. Zero has no inverse, and neither do 2, 3 and 4, because their products with everything are even or zero and never 1.

15
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Addition modulo 6 passes on every row. The inverse of a is 6 minus a, and 0 is its own inverse. That structure is a group.

16
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
So a group is a monoid in which every element has an inverse. Same set, two operations, two different answers: one monoid, one group.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Commutativity says a star b equals b star a. In the table it is a statement about the whole picture: the cell in row a column b must match the cell in row b column a.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Now the six permutations of three letters under composition. This is S3, the symmetric group, and it has six elements like Z6 does.

19
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Two of them fail to commute. Swapping the first two letters and then cycling gives a different permutation from cycling and then swapping.

20
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
An abelian group is a group whose table is symmetric. Z6 is abelian; S3 is a group and not abelian; both have order 6.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
The order of an element is the smallest positive power that returns the identity. In the units of 7, the powers of 2 are 2, 4, 1, so 2 has order 3.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:46,000
If some element has order equal to the size of the group, it generates everything and the group is cyclic. Here 3 generates all six units of 7.

23
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Two groups of order 4. The integers modulo 4 have an element of order 4. The units of 8 have only orders 1 and 2, so no single element generates the group.

24
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
The order of an element always divides the order of the group. That single sentence is the reason most subgroup questions can be answered by arithmetic.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
A subgroup is a subset that is itself a group under the same operation. To test it: the identity is inside, the subset is closed, and every element inside has its inverse inside.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Addition modulo 6 has four subgroups: zero alone, zero with 3, zero with 2 and 4, and the whole group. Sizes 1, 2, 3 and 6.

27
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Shift the subgroup by each element and you get cosets. Each coset has the same size, and together they split the group with nothing left over.

28
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
The number of cosets is the index, and the group size is the index times the subgroup size. So the subgroup size divides the group size.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Lagrange's theorem: the order of a subgroup divides the order of the group. A group of 35 elements can only have subgroups of size 1, 5, 7 or 35.

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:46,000
The same one-line test answers the other sizes. For 84 the largest proper divisor is 42, and for 15 with a subgroup of at least 4 elements the only candidate is 5.

31
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Now the warning. The alternating group on four letters has 12 elements, and six divides twelve, but it has no subgroup of order 6.

32
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
In a cyclic group the converse does hold: one subgroup for each divisor. The integers modulo 12 have six divisors and exactly six subgroups.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
If the order is prime, every non-identity element has order equal to the whole group, so every non-identity element generates it. Every group of prime order is cyclic.

34
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Order 6 is the first case where the answer is not forced. There are exactly two groups of order 6 up to renaming: Z6, which is cyclic, and S3, which is not.

35
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:24,000
So if H is a subgroup with size strictly between 1 and 6, its size is 2 or 3. Both are prime, so H is cyclic in either group.

36
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
And two groups of order 6 cannot be the same group when one has an element of order 6 and the other does not. Element orders are an isomorphism fingerprint.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,000
For a classification question, climb the ladder in order and stop at the first rung that fails: closed, associative, identity, inverses, commutative.

38
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:46,000
The power set of a three-element set under symmetric difference: the empty set is the identity, every set is its own inverse, and the order of the whole group is 8.

39
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Functions from the non-negative integers to themselves under pointwise addition: closed, associative, commutative, identity is the zero function. But no inverse, so a monoid.

40
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
The integers under multiplication stop at the same rung: 1 is the identity, and 2 has no inverse among the integers. Multiplication is a monoid, not a group.

41
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:08,000
One: the verdict belongs to the pair of set and operation. Name the operation before you answer, and check closure first.

42
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Two: for any subgroup question on a finite group, start with Lagrange. The size divides the order, so list the divisors and read the answer off.

43
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:24,000
Three: the order of an element divides the group order, and a group is cyclic exactly when some element has order equal to the group size.

44
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:30,000
Four: know the two groups of order 6. Z6 is cyclic and abelian; S3 is neither, and every proper non-trivial subgroup of either is cyclic.

